1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

Spatiotemporal dynamical systems—from climate dynamics and fluid turbulence to neural activity and epidemic spread—are ubiquitous in science and engineering. In almost all real‑world settings we have only a sparse set of point‑wise sensors (weather stations, probes, electrodes), and the governing partial differential equations (PDEs) are unknown or too expensive to simulate. Extracting from such measurements a model that is simultaneously predictive, interpretable, causal, stable, and uncertainty‑aware constitutes a grand challenge for data‑driven digital twins.

1.2 Recent Progress and Remaining Gaps

Recent breakthroughs in the SHRED family (Williams et al., 2022) learn to reconstruct spatiotemporal fields from a sliding window of sensor histories using a GRU encoder and shallow decoder. SINDy‑SHRED (Gao, Williams & Kutz, 2025) added sparse equation discovery inside the latent space. T‑SHRED (2025) introduced a Transformer with SINDy‑attention; SHRED‑ROM (2025) enabled parametric reduced‑order modelling; and UQ‑SHRED (2026) provided engression‑based distributional outputs. Despite these impressive achievements, all existing variants share fundamental limitations:

1. Fixed Symbolic Libraries. The candidate function set is static; unseen physical terms are permanently missed.
2. No Formal Stability Guarantees. Heuristic penalties cannot certify that long‑term rollouts remain bounded.
3. Absence of Causal Structure. The learned ODE is a black‑box coupling; no directed causal graph is extracted.
4. Limited Uncertainty Quantification. UQ‑SHRED provides no rigorous coverage guarantees for multi‑step forecasts.
5. Theoretical Gaps. Fundamental barriers (Colbrook et al., 2024; Aliee et al., 2025) show finite‑dimensional Koopman invariance and ODE identifiability are impossible without strong assumptions.

1.3 Contributions of ACKS‑WM

ACKS‑WM overcomes these gaps while extending the SHRED architecture. Its core intellectual contributions are:

· Stochastic MDL‑Controlled Adaptive Library (Section 4.2). The library expands online only when a new term reduces the residual by more than the ensemble’s predictive uncertainty, preventing noise‑driven over‑expansion.
· Physically Anchored Causal Discovery (Section 4.5). A partial identity pinning forces the first latent dimensions to reconstruct sensor signals, anchoring the discovered causal graph to real spatial locations.
· Triggered Formal Stability Certification (Section 4.6). Barrier‑focussed counterexample sampling triggers expensive α,β‑CROWN verification only when the Lyapunov residual and Koopman invariance gap are both “stability‑ready”, enabling practical certification.
· Manifold‑Freezing Curriculum (Section 5). During library expansions, the encoder is temporarily frozen to eliminate destructive gradient interference with the Lyapunov penalty.
· Conformal Uncertainty Quantification (Section 4.4). Ensemble‑SINDy with conformal calibration guarantees exact one‑step marginal coverage and asymptotic multi‑step coverage.
· Rigorous Theoretical Analysis (Section 6). A suite of theorems provides identifiability, certified stability, causal graph recovery, noise‑tolerant termination, and quantitative Koopman error bounds. All proofs are self‑contained.

1.4 Scope and Status

This chapter presents the theoretical and algorithmic design, together with complete proofs. No experimental results are reported; planned benchmarks and metrics are described in Section 8. Implementation and empirical validation will form the next phase of this doctoral research.

