2. Related Work

2.1 Sparse Sensor Reconstruction – The SHRED Ecosystem
SHRED (Williams et al., 2022) uses a GRU encoder and shallow decoder to reconstruct fields from sensor histories. SINDy‑SHRED (Gao, Williams & Kutz, 2025) adds a SINDy regulariser, yielding joint field reconstruction and sparse governing equation discovery. T‑SHRED (2025) replaces the GRU with a Transformer; SHRED‑ROM (2025) enables parametric reduced‑order modelling; UQ‑SHRED (2026) introduces engression‑based distributional learning. None of these methods incorporate an adaptive library, formal stability certificate, or explicit causal graph recovery.

2.2 Equation Discovery and Adaptive Symbolic Regression
SINDy (Brunton et al., 2016) discovers sparse governing equations via ℓ₁‑regularised regression over a fixed library. SINDy‑autoencoders (Champion et al., 2019) learn latent coordinates simultaneously. Bayesian SINDy autoencoders (Gao & Kutz, 2024) and Ensemble‑SINDy (Fasel et al., 2022) improve robustness. Adaptive approaches, e.g., SyMANTIC (Mutthyala et al., 2025), expand the library based on information‑theoretic criteria. ACKS‑WM’s expansion is driven by a direct residual threshold and regulated by an S‑MDL penalty that explicitly accounts for predictive uncertainty.

2.3 Koopman Operator Theory and Diagnostics
Deep Koopman methods (Lusch et al., 2018) learn finite‑dimensional linear embeddings. Colbrook et al. (2024) proved that a finite invariant subspace is impossible for many systems. Conradie, Boullé, Loiseau, Brunton & Colbrook (2026) recently introduced a posteriori diagnostics: the principal angle (invariance proximity) \theta_1 between the lifted subspace and its Koopman image quantifies the irreducible error, and multi‑step forecasting error grows linearly with \sin\theta_1. ACKS‑WM exploits these diagnostics for dictionary refinement and verification gating.

2.4 Causal Discovery in Dynamical Systems
CausalKinetiX (Pfister et al., 2019) recovers causal differential equations from interventional data. In latent spaces, CITRIS (Lippe et al., 2022) and Lachapelle et al. (2022) establish identifiability under soft interventions. Aliee et al. (2025) proved that governing ODEs are non‑identifiable in general. ACKS‑WM uses in silico additive shifts as soft interventions; its causal identifiability theorem is placed within this modern framework, and strengthened by physical pinning.

2.5 Formal Stability via Verified Lyapunov Functions
Neural Lyapunov functions (Meng et al., 2025) can be formally verified with δ‑complete SMT solvers (dReal). α,β‑CROWN (Zhang et al., 2025) provides orders‑of‑magnitude speedup for continuous‑time verification. Compositionally verifiable vector Lyapunov functions (IEEE TAC, 2024) scale to high dimensions under weak coupling. ACKS‑WM adopts a triggered verification protocol that drastically reduces total computation while preserving guarantees.

2.6 Uncertainty Quantification with Conformal Prediction
Fasel et al. (2025) integrated conformal prediction with Ensemble‑SINDy, achieving exact marginal coverage for time‑series forecasts. For multi‑step predictions, block‑conformal (Chernozhukov et al., 2018) or adaptive conformal inference (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) provide valid coverage under dependence. ACKS‑WM grounds its UQ module in these methods.

