3. Formal Problem Setting

3.1 True System and Galerkin Truncation

Let the true state belong to a Hilbert space \mathcal{H}. The evolution is governed by an unknown smooth vector field. Through a Galerkin truncation retaining d degrees of freedom, the discretised state u(t)\in\mathbb{R}^d satisfies

\frac{du}{dt} = F_{\text{disc}}(u(t)), \qquad u(0)=u_0 .

For PDEs with sufficiently smooth solutions, classical spectral error bounds give \|u_{\text{true}} - u_{\text{disc}}\|_{C^1} = \mathcal{O}(d^{-r}) for some r>0. We assume d is large enough that truncation error is negligible relative to sensor noise.

3.2 Sensor Model

Sparse sensors provide m\ll d noisy measurements

y(t) = h(u(t)) + \eta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^m, \qquad \|\eta(t)\|_2 \le \eta_{\max},

where h is a known observation function (e.g., point‑wise evaluation).

3.3 Observability Assumption

We assume the pair (F_{\text{disc}}, h) satisfies the n-step observability rank condition, i.e., the extended observability map \mathcal{O}_n(u) = (h(u), L_{F_{\text{disc}}}h(u), \dots, L_{F_{\text{disc}}}^{n-1}h(u)) is an injective immersion of the attractor onto a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^{mn} for generic initial conditions. This guarantees that a sufficiently long sensor history determines the full state, and a well‑designed neural encoder can learn an invertible mapping from histories to a latent space \mathcal{Z}\cong\mathbb{R}^n with n=d.

3.4 Goals

From a finite training set of sensor sequences and ground‑truth full‑field snapshots (used only for supervision), we aim to learn:

· G1 A minimal latent manifold \mathcal{Z}\cong\mathbb{R}^n and a diffeomorphic encoder–decoder pair.
· G2 A sparse, symbolic ODE \dot{z}=g(z) approximating the true latent dynamics up to a diffeomorphism.
· G3 A Koopman‑motivated lifted decomposition \dot\Theta = A\Theta + \mathcal{N}(\Theta) with a quantified invariance gap.
· G4 A decoder with predictive uncertainty and conformal coverage guarantees.
· G5 A directed acyclic causal graph among latent variables, physically anchored when possible, identifiable under soft interventions.
· G6 A formal stability guarantee for low‑dimensional subsystems, extendable compositionally.

