4. ACKS‑WM Architecture

4.1 Neural Observer with Optional Physical Anchoring

A continuous‑time ODE–GRU processes the sensor history and outputs a hidden state from which a linear projection yields the latent state z(t)\in\mathbb{R}^n. To anchor the causal graph to physical sensor locations, we may enforce that the first m latent coordinates directly reconstruct the m sensor readings via an identity pinning loss

\mathcal{L}_{\text{pin}} = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \|z_i(t) - y_i(t)\|^2 .

When active, this forces z_{1:m} to be an auto‑encoding of the sensor signals, so that causal edges among these coordinates can be directly interpreted as physical sensor interactions.

4.2 Adaptive Symbolic Latent Dynamics with Stochastic MDL (S‑MDL)

The latent dynamics are modelled as

\frac{dz}{dt} = g(z) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \xi_k \,\phi_k(z), \qquad \phi_k\in\mathcal{L}.

The initial library \mathcal{L}_0 contains a small set (e.g., monomials up to degree 2 and basic trigonometric terms). During training we monitor the temporal residual \bar{r} = \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \|\dot{z} - g(z)\|_2\,dt. If \bar{r} exceeds a threshold \tau, we consider expanding \mathcal{L} by forming new candidate functions from the active set (products, powers, trigonometric combinations).

Naïve expansion driven purely by a residual threshold is vulnerable to noise: random fluctuations can simulate error reduction, triggering unnecessary expansions that degrade interpretability. We therefore introduce a stochastic MDL criterion. Let \sigma_{\text{dyn}} be the empirical standard deviation of the residual \|\dot{z} - g(z)\| across the ensemble members. A candidate expansion is accepted only if the expected residual reduction satisfies \Delta\bar{r} > \kappa \sigma_{\text{dyn}} (with \kappa \approx 1). The MDL penalty is also augmented:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{S-MDL}} = \alpha\sum_k\log(1+|\xi_k|) + \beta\cdot\mathrm{nnz}(\Xi) + \lambda_{\text{var}}\,\mathbb{I}[\Delta\bar{r} \le \sigma_{\text{dyn}}].

This ensures that library complexity grows only when the signal‑to‑noise ratio justifies it. The S‑MDL criterion and the finite size of the candidate space together guarantee that the expansion terminates in finitely many steps with high probability (Proposition 6.4).

4.3 Linear‑Dominant Lifted Dynamics with Koopman Diagnostics

We learn a lifting \Theta:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^N (N>n) and decompose

\frac{d}{dt}\Theta(z) = A\,\Theta(z) + \mathcal{N}(\Theta(z)),

where A is the best L^2-projection onto \mathrm{span}(\Theta) and \mathcal{N} is a sparse nonlinear residual. The irreducible approximation error is measured by the invariance proximity \sin\theta_1, the sine of the principal angle between \mathrm{span}(\Theta) and its Koopman image. As shown by Conradie et al. (2026), the k-step forecasting error satisfies

\|\Theta(z(k\Delta t)) - A^k\Theta(z_0)\| \le C\,k\,\sin\theta_1\,\|\Theta(z_0)\|,

i.e., error grows linearly with the rollout length scaled by the invariance gap. We monitor \sin\theta_1 after training and trigger dictionary refinement only when it exceeds a predefined tolerance and the Lyapunov function is stable (Section 4.6).

4.4 Probabilistic Decoder with Conformal UQ

The decoder outputs a mean field \mu(x,t) and a log‑standard‑deviation field, modelling the full‑field likelihood as conditionally Gaussian. We train a small ensemble (e.g., 5 members) with different initialisations and data subsampling. The ensemble not only captures epistemic uncertainty but also provides \sigma_{\text{dyn}} for the S‑MDL criterion. After training, split‑conformal calibration on a held‑out set adjusts prediction intervals to achieve the user‑specified marginal coverage 1-\alpha.

· For one‑step‑ahead predictions, exchangeability guarantees exact marginal coverage: \mathbb{P}(y_{t+1}\in\hat{C}_{t+1}) \ge 1-\alpha.
· For multi‑step rollouts under β‑mixing, we employ block‑conformal or adaptive conformal inference, giving asymptotically valid coverage.

4.5 Causal Intervention Module with Physical Anchoring

We perform in silico soft interventions by adding independent random shifts \delta_i to the latent initial condition z(0). In separate training episodes, for each coordinate i, we set \delta_i\sim\text{Uniform}[-\Delta,\Delta] and \delta_{j\neq i}=0. The intervened trajectory follows \dot{z}_\delta = g(z_\delta). The causal invariance loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{causal}} = \mathbb{E}_{\delta}\Big[ \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t}\|\dot{z}_\delta(t) - g(z_\delta(t))\|_1\Big].

From the sparse coefficient matrix of g, we extract a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where edge j\to i exists iff a term involving z_j appears in the equation for \dot{z}_i. When identity pinning is active, the first m nodes correspond directly to the physical sensors; interventions on z_i ($i\le m$) correspond to perturbing the representation of a specific sensor, and the discovered graph directly maps sensor‑level causal interactions.

4.6 Triggered Formal Stability via Barrier‑Focussed Sampling

For a low‑dimensional subsystem \mathcal{D}_k\subset\mathbb{R}^k (k\le 10), we learn a neural Lyapunov function V_\phi(z) and enforce the decrease condition

\nabla V_\phi(z)\cdot g(z) \le -\gamma V_\phi(z).

Formal verification with δ‑complete SMT solvers is too expensive to run every epoch. Instead, we adopt a barrier‑focussed sampling strategy:

· During training, dReal identifies counterexamples – states where \dot{V}_\phi > -\gamma V_\phi. These critical points are collected in a safety buffer.
· Every p epochs, we compute two readiness metrics: the maximum Lyapunov residue on the buffer, R_{\text{Lyap}} = \max_{\text{buffer}}(\dot{V}_\phi + \gamma V_\phi), and the invariance proximity \sin\theta_1.
· Full α,β‑CROWN verification is triggered only when both metrics fall below predetermined “stability‑ready” thresholds: R_{\text{Lyap}} < \epsilon_{\text{Lyap}} and \sin\theta_1 < \epsilon_{\text{inv}}.

This strategy decouples rapid empirical falsification (cheap) from expensive formal certification (rare), reducing total verification time by an estimated factor of 10 while preserving the guarantee that once certified, the Lyapunov function is valid for all states in \mathcal{D}_k.

If the causal graph reveals that the full latent system can be decomposed into weakly coupled subsystems, we apply compositional verification (IEEE TAC, 2024) to extend the certificate to the high‑dimensional space.

