6. Theoretical Analysis

We present the rigorous foundation of the framework’s core guarantees. All proofs are self‑contained and make explicit the required assumptions.

6.1 Assumptions

Assumption 6.1 (Observability). The pair (F_{\text{disc}}, h) satisfies the n-step observability rank condition on the attractor, with n=d.

Assumption 6.2 (Excitation and Mutual Coherence). For the latent trajectory data and dictionary \Phi_{ik}=\phi_k(z(t_i)), the mutual coherence \mu(\Phi) < 1/(2s-1), where s is the true sparsity. We estimate s conservatively as \hat{s}=\mathrm{nnz}(\Xi_{\text{prev}})+1 after each expansion and abort expansion if the updated dictionary violates this bound.

Assumption 6.3 (Modularity and Soft Intervention Diversity). The latent dynamics are modular: \dot{z}_i = g_i(z_{\text{Pa}(i)}). The in silico soft interventions are independent additive shifts, providing sufficient diversity to separate parental sets (Lachapelle et al., 2022).

Assumption 6.4 (Noise Model). The measurement noise \eta(t) is mean‑zero Gaussian with variance \sigma^2, and the dynamics residual noise inherited by \dot{z} is sub‑Gaussian with variance proxy \sigma^2_{\text{dyn}}.

6.2 Structural Identifiability of Latent Dynamics

Theorem 6.1 (Local Diffeomorphic Identifiability with MDL‑Sparsity).
Under Assumptions 6.1 and 6.2, and assuming the final library \mathcal{L} is symbolically complete, the coefficient vector \Xi is the unique sparsest solution to \dot{z}=\Phi\Xi on the data manifold. Within the fixed latent coordinate system provided by the encoder, the S‑MDL penalty selects the unique sparsest coefficient vector. Consequently, there exists a diffeomorphism \Phi:\mathcal{Z}\to\mathcal{M} such that g = \Phi^* F_{\text{disc}} on the attractor.

Proof. The encoder yields a diffeomorphism from history windows to \mathcal{Z} (Assumption 6.1). The dynamics loss forces g to equal the push‑forward of F_{\text{disc}}. Under mutual coherence \mu(\Phi)<1/(2s-1), the sparsest solution is the unique minimizer of the constrained ℓ₁ problem (Donoho & Elad, 2003). Zhang & Schaeffer (2019, Lemma 2, Theorem 5) proved that the iterative thresholding algorithm used in SINDy converges to this solution under the same condition. Hence the coefficient vector found by ACKS‑WM’s sparse regression is exactly the sparsest representer of the dynamics.

Now, all vectors with the same support that achieve the same data‑fit (to within noise) are equivalent in terms of ℒ_data. The S‑MDL penalty includes a model‑coding cost that is strictly convex in the coefficient magnitudes via the universal prior for integers. Therefore, among the finitely many sparsest solutions the MDL selects a unique optimum.

Remark. The diffeomorphism class ambiguity is not resolved by MDL; it is encoded in the choice of encoder architecture and initialisation, a standard limitation of SINDy‑autoencoders (Champion et al., 2019). The theorem guarantees uniqueness relative to the learned coordinates.

6.3 Certified Local Exponential Boundedness

Theorem 6.2 (Local Exponential Boundedness).
Let V_\phi be a neural Lyapunov function that, on a compact set \mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{R}^n containing the training attractor, satisfies V_\phi(z)>0 for z\neq0 and \nabla V_\phi(z)\cdot g(z) \le -\gamma V_\phi(z) for all z\in\mathcal{D}. Then any trajectory starting in a sublevel set \{z:V_\phi(z)\le c\}\subset\mathcal{D} remains in \mathcal{D} and converges exponentially to the attractor. If the Lyapunov conditions have been formally verified on the low‑dimensional subsystem via α,β‑CROWN, the guarantee is unconditional on that domain. For the full latent space, the bound holds up to the optimality of the learned Lyapunov function. When the causal graph reveals weak coupling, compositional verification can extend the formal certificate to higher dimensions.

Proof. For z(0)\in S_c = \{z:V_\phi(z)\le c\}\subset\mathcal{D}, while the trajectory stays in \mathcal{D},

\frac{d}{dt}V_\phi(z(t)) = \nabla V_\phi \cdot g \le -\gamma V_\phi .

Gronwall’s inequality gives V_\phi(z(t)) \le V_\phi(z(0))e^{-\gamma t} \le c e^{-\gamma t}, so the state never leaves S_c. Exponential convergence follows. The triggered verification strategy ensures that when a certificate is issued, the condition holds for all z in the verified domain (dReal yields unsat; α,β‑CROWN guarantees the bound holds at every point), making the result unconditional for that subsystem.

6.4 Causal Graph Identifiability with Physical Anchoring

Theorem 6.3 (Causal Graph Identifiability under Soft Interventions with Pinning).
Under Assumption 6.3, if \mathcal{L}_{\text{causal}}=0 on the training distribution and the first m latent coordinates are pinned to the m sensor signals via \mathcal{L}_{\text{pin}}, then the support of the Jacobian J_g (the adjacency matrix) is identifiable up to a permutation of the pinned nodes and a component‑wise diffeomorphism that preserves the sparsity pattern. In particular, among the first m coordinates, the set of non‑zero coefficients in the symbolic equation for each \dot{z}_i identifies the direct physical‑sensor causes of the i-th sensor.

Proof. The causal loss enforces invariance of the conditional distribution of \dot{z}_i | z_{\text{Pa}(i)} under shifts to non‑parent variables. This is the soft‑intervention setup of Lachapelle et al. (2022), who proved that sparsity in the mechanism g_i plus sufficient interventional diversity identifies the support of the causal graph up to a permutation and component‑wise nonlinearity (Theorem 4.2). Because our interventions are additive initial‑condition shifts, they propagate through the dynamics but, under modularity, only affect the equations of children nodes. The ℓ₁ penalty encourages minimal support, which under the true modularity equals the true parent set. Hence the adjacency matrix is recovered up to the stated equivalence.

Identity pinning fixes the meaning of the first m coordinates: they directly represent the sensor signals. Therefore, any permutation of these nodes corresponds to a permutation of the physical sensors, which is trivially interpretable (relabelling of sensors). The sparsity‑pattern‑preserving diffeomorphism respects the zero‑non‑zero structure, so the causal graph edges among pinned nodes correspond to physical sensor‑influence relationships.

Remark. This result concerns the graph of the learned latent ODE. Physical validity is guaranteed only for the pinned dimensions; unpinned dimensions remain mathematically correct but physically uninterpretable until mapped to a known basis.

6.5 Noise‑Tolerant Termination of Library Expansion

Proposition 6.4 (Finite Termination under S‑MDL with High Probability).
Let the dynamics residual noise be sub‑Gaussian with variance proxy \sigma_{\text{dyn}}^2. The S‑MDL expansion criterion accepts a candidate term only if the residual reduction \Delta\bar{r} > \kappa\sigma_{\text{dyn}} for some \kappa>0. Then, with probability at least 1-\delta, the library expansion halts after a finite number of steps that depends only on the true signal complexity and the noise level.

Proof. Suppose the true dynamics are exactly spanned by a finite set of basis functions \mathcal{L}^* of size K^*. For any candidate term \phi not in \mathcal{L}^*, the true additional residual reduction is zero. The empirical reduction \Delta\bar{r} computed from T samples is an average of squared residuals; under sub‑Gaussian noise, it concentrates around zero with \mathbb{P}(\Delta\bar{r} > \epsilon) \le C_1\exp(-C_2 T \epsilon^2/\sigma_{\text{dyn}}^2). Choosing \epsilon = \kappa\sigma_{\text{dyn}} gives a tail probability that decays exponentially in T. Applying a union bound over all possible expansions (which is finite because the total candidate space for bounded degree is finite) shows that with probability 1-\delta no redundant term is ever added beyond K^*. The S‑MDL criterion thus rejects all noise‑driven expansions, and the library size remains bounded. \square

6.6 Koopman Multi‑Step Error Bound (Corrected)

Theorem 6.5 (Linear Error Growth with Invariance Gap).
Let \theta_1 be the principal angle between \mathrm{span}(\Theta) and its Koopman image. Then for the lifted linear model A,

\|\Theta(z(k\Delta t)) - A^k\Theta(z_0)\| \le C\,k\,\sin\theta_1\,\|\Theta(z_0)\|,

where C depends on the bounded nonlinear residual \mathcal{N}. As \sin\theta_1\to0, the linear model becomes exact.

Proof. Write the exact lifted dynamics as \Theta(z_{j+1}) = A\Theta(z_j) + \varepsilon_j, where \varepsilon_j is the projection error onto the orthogonal complement of \mathrm{span}(\Theta). Conradie et al. (2026, Theorem 4.1) bound \|\varepsilon_j\| \le C_1 \sin\theta_1 \|\Theta(z_j)\|. Iterating k times gives

\Theta(z_k) - A^k\Theta(z_0) = \sum_{\ell=0}^{k-1} A^{k-1-\ell} \varepsilon_\ell .

Since \|A\|\le 1 (enforced during training by spectral regularisation), each term satisfies \|A^{k-1-\ell}\varepsilon_\ell\| \le \|\varepsilon_\ell\|. Using the bound on \varepsilon_\ell and the fact that the Lyapunov‑stabilized dynamics keep \|\Theta(z_\ell)\| uniformly bounded by M\|\Theta(z_0)\|, the sum is at most k C_1 M \sin\theta_1 \|\Theta(z_0)\|, proving the result.

6.7 Conformal Coverage Guarantees

Theorem 6.6 (Conformal Coverage).
(a) For one‑step‑ahead predictions, the split‑conformal predictor achieves exact marginal coverage \mathbb{P}(y_{t+1}\in\hat{C}_{t+1}) \ge 1-\alpha.
(b) For multi‑step rollouts under β‑mixing, block‑conformal or adaptive conformal inference yields asymptotically valid coverage.

Proof sketch. (a) Standard split‑conformal argument (Vovk et al., 2005; Fasel et al., 2025). (b) Block‑conformal (Chernozhukov et al., 2018) groups consecutive residuals into blocks that are approximately exchangeable under mixing; adaptive conformal inference (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) incrementally updates quantiles. Both provide coverage approaching 1-\alpha as T\to\infty. \square

