Appendix A — Discretisation Error Bound (KdV Equation)

We sketch the argument showing that a Galerkin truncation of the Korteweg–de Vries (KdV) equation induces a vector‑field error that decays with the number of retained modes. The same ideas apply to other PDEs with analytic solutions.

Consider the KdV equation on a periodic domain x\in[0,2\pi]:

u_t + u u_x + u_{xxx} = 0 .

Expand the solution in a Fourier series: u(x,t)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} \hat{u}_k(t) e^{i k x}. The projected dynamics for a truncated set |k|\le K is obtained by the standard Galerkin projection. The difference between the infinite‑dimensional vector field F_{\text{true}} and its truncated counterpart F_{\text{disc}} is the residual of the truncated modes.

For analytic initial data, \|\hat{u}_k(0)\|\le C e^{-a|k|} for some a>0. In the H^s topology, the truncation error satisfies

\|u_{\text{true}} - u_{\text{disc}}\|_{H^s}^2 = \sum_{|k|>K} (1+|k|^2)^s |\hat{u}_k|^2 = \mathcal{O}(K^{2s} e^{-2aK}).

With d=2K+1 degrees of freedom, one has d^{-r} polynomial decay (or exponential if analyticity persists). The Sobolev embedding H^s\subset C^1 for s>3/2 yields a C^1 bound on the vector field error of the same order. For finite d chosen large, this truncation error is negligible compared with sensor noise. Consequently, the latent dynamics learned on the attractor of F_{\text{disc}} approximate those of the true PDE with an irreducible model‑form error \mathcal{O}(d^{-r}).

