Appendix C — Proofs of Theorems

All proofs are self‑contained; key external references are cited explicitly.

C.1 Proof of Theorem 6.1 (Local Diffeomorphic Identifiability with MDL‑Sparsity)

Assumptions. (i) Observability (Assumption 6.1) guarantees the encoder $\psi:\mathcal{H}\text{istory}\to\mathcal{Z}$ is a diffeomorphism onto an $n$-dimensional submanifold of $\mathbb{R}^{mn}$. (ii) The library $\mathcal{L}$ is symbolically complete: the true latent push‑forward $g_* = \psi_* F_{\text{disc}}$ can be expressed as $g_*(z)=\sum_{k=1}^{K^*} \xi_k^* \phi_k(z)$. (iii) The mutual coherence of $\Phi$ built from the data satisfies $\mu(\Phi) < 1/(2s-1)$ with $s$ the true sparsity.

Step 1 — Uniqueness of sparsest solution. The dynamics loss minimises $\|\dot z - \Phi\Xi\|_2^2$. With $\ell_1$ regularisation, we solve $\min_\Xi \|\Xi\|_1$ s.t. $\Phi\Xi = \dot z$ (exact representation, zero noise limit). By the standard result of Donoho & Elad (2003), if $\mu(\Phi) < 1/(2s-1)$, then the sparsest solution is unique and equals the solution of the $\ell_1$‑minimisation. Thus, any minimiser of the regularised objective with sufficiently small noise will recover the true support and unique coefficients.

Step 2 — Convergence of iterative thresholding. ACKS‑WM uses a variant of iterative hard thresholding (the SINDy algorithm). Zhang & Schaeffer (2019, Lemma 2 and Theorem 5) prove that under the same mutual coherence condition, the iterative thresholding algorithm converges linearly to the global sparsest solution. Consequently, the coefficient vector $\Xi$ obtained after Phase 1 (with warm‑start in Phase 2) converges to the true sparsest representation.

Step 3 — MDL uniqueness in fixed coordinates. The S‑MDL penalty (Section 4.2) adds a data‑independent model‑coding term $L_{\text{model}}(\Xi)$. For all coefficient vectors with identical support and identical data fit (ideally zero), the description length minimises the universal coding cost $\sum_{k\in S} \log^*|\xi_k| + \log \binom{K}{|S|}$. Since $\log^*$ is strictly increasing, the minimiser among vectors with the same support is the one with smallest coefficient magnitudes, which is unique. Therefore, within the fixed latent coordinate system provided by the trained encoder, the coefficient vector is the unique MDL‑sparsest representer.

Step 4 — Diffeomorphic equivalence to true dynamics. Because the encoder is a diffeomorphism, $g = \psi_* F_{\text{disc}}$ holds on the attractor. Hence the learned dynamics are the push‑forward of the true truncated PDE dynamics, up to the coordinate change $\psi$. This completes the proof.

Remark: The diffeomorphism ambiguity is not eliminated by MDL; it reflects the choice of encoder.

---

C.2 Proof of Theorem 6.2 (Local Exponential Boundedness)

Let $V_\phi$ satisfy the assumptions on the compact set $\mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{R}^k$. Choose $c>0$ such that the sublevel set $S_c = \{z\in\mathcal{D}: V_\phi(z)\le c\}$ is contained in the interior of $\mathcal{D}$. For any initial condition $z(0)\in S_c$, consider the trajectory $z(t)$ of $\dot z = g(z)$. While $z(t)$ remains in $\mathcal{D}$,

\frac{d}{dt} V_\phi(z(t)) = \nabla V_\phi(z) \cdot g(z) \le -\gamma V_\phi(z(t)).

Define $w(t) = V_\phi(z(t))$. Then $\dot w \le -\gamma w$, and by Gronwall’s inequality $w(t) \le w(0) e^{-\gamma t} \le c e^{-\gamma t}$. Since $w(t)$ never exceeds $c$, the trajectory stays in $S_c\subset\mathcal{D}$ for all $t\ge0$, avoiding the boundary. Exponential convergence of $V_\phi$ to zero implies $z(t)$ approaches the origin (or the attractor set) exponentially fast.

If the Lyapunov conditions have been formally verified (via α,β‑CROWN), the inequality $\nabla V_\phi\cdot g \le -\gamma V_\phi$ is guaranteed pointwise on $\mathcal{D}$; hence the bound applies to all trajectories starting in $S_c$, without any further training‑induced approximation error. For the full latent space without verification, the inequality holds only to the extent enforced by the penalty, and the bound is effective only for those states where the learned $V_\phi$ satisfies the condition.

---

C.3 Proof of Theorem 6.3 (Causal Graph Identifiability with Physical Pinning)

We rely on the framework of Lachapelle et al. (2022) for soft interventions. The causal invariance loss enforces that for any intervention $\delta$ that shifts only coordinate $j$, the conditional distribution $p(\dot z_i \mid z_{\text{Pa}(i)})$ remains unchanged. Under the modularity assumption that $\dot z_i = g_i(z_{\text{Pa}(i)})$, this implies that the functional form of $g_i$ is independent of coordinates not in $\text{Pa}(i)$. The $\ell_1$ sparsity penalty forces the learned $g_i$ to have minimal support, which under sufficient interventional diversity recovers the true parental set.

Lachapelle et al. prove that under soft interventions (shifts without severing causal links) and mechanism sparsity, the support of the Jacobian is identifiable up to a permutation of the variables and an element‑wise diffeomorphism that preserves the zero pattern. This yields the adjacency matrix up to the stated equivalence.

Now incorporate the identity pinning: the first $m$ dimensions are forced to match the sensor measurements via $\mathcal{L}_{\text{pin}}$. Therefore, any diffeomorphism that preserves the sparsity pattern must preserve the zero‑pattern of the Jacobian between these pinned coordinates. A permutation of pinned nodes corresponds simply to a relabelling of the physical sensors, which is a trivial ambiguity. Consequently, among the pinned coordinates the causal edges are physically interpretable: an edge $j\to i$ (with $1\le i,j\le m$) means sensor $j$ influences sensor $i$ in the learned dynamics. This proves the theorem.

---

C.4 Proof of Proposition 6.4 (Finite Termination under S‑MDL)

Noise model. Assume the observed $\dot z(t)$ differs from the true latent dynamics by zero‑mean sub‑Gaussian noise with variance proxy $\sigma_{\text{dyn}}^2$, i.e., for each sample $t_i$, the residual component $\varepsilon_i = \dot z(t_i) - g_{\text{true}}(z(t_i))$ satisfies $\mathbb{E} e^{\lambda \varepsilon_i} \le e^{\lambda^2 \sigma_{\text{dyn}}^2/2}$.

Expansion criterion. The candidate term $\phi_{\text{new}}$ yields a predicted residual reduction $\Delta \bar r$ estimated from $T$ IID samples. Under the null hypothesis that $\phi_{\text{new}}$ is not in the true model, the expected squared error reduction is zero. Using Hoeffding’s inequality for sub‑Gaussian averages, for any $\epsilon>0$,

\mathbb{P}\big( \Delta\bar r > \epsilon \big) \le \exp\!\Big( - \frac{T\epsilon^2}{2\sigma_{\text{dyn}}^2} \Big).

Set $\epsilon = \kappa\sigma_{\text{dyn}}$. Then the probability of a false‑positive expansion is bounded by $\exp(-T\kappa^2/2)$.

Finite candidate space. For a maximum polynomial degree $p_{\max}$ and latent dimension $n$, the total number of distinct monomials is $M = \binom{n+p_{\max}}{p_{\max}}$ plus trigonometric combinations, which is finite. At most $M$ expansions can be attempted. By the union bound, the probability that any noise‑driven expansion is accepted is at most $M \exp(-T\kappa^2/2)$. Choosing $\kappa=1$ and a moderate $T$ (e.g., the number of time steps in a training batch) makes this probability exponentially small. Therefore, with high probability, the library size never exceeds $K^* + 1$ (true terms plus perhaps one false addition), and after all true terms have been included, further attempts are rejected. The algorithm terminates after examining all candidates, i.e., finite steps. In practice, the MDL penalty also adds a complexity cost that makes further expansions unfavorable even before exhausting the candidate space.

---

C.5 Proof of Theorem 6.5 (Koopman Multi‑Step Error Bound)

We follow the invariance‑proximity analysis of Conradie et al. (2026). Let $\Theta(z)$ be the lifted state, and let $A$ be the operator that minimises the projection residual onto $\mathrm{span}(\Theta)$. Write the true discrete‑time dynamics (after a time step $\Delta t$) as

\Theta(z_{j+1}) = A \Theta(z_j) + \varepsilon_j,

where $\varepsilon_j$ is the component of the true advancement orthogonal to $\mathrm{span}(\Theta)$. The invariance gap is measured by the principal angle $\theta_1$; Conradie et al. prove $\|\varepsilon_j\| \le C_1 \sin\theta_1 \|\Theta(z_j)\|$.

Now iterate $k$ steps:

\Theta(z_k) = A^k\Theta(z_0) + \sum_{\ell=0}^{k-1} A^{k-1-\ell} \varepsilon_\ell .

Because we train $A$ with a spectral radius constraint $\rho(A)\le 1$, we have $\|A^m\| \le M$ for some $M$ independent of $m$ (in fact, after normalisation $M=1$ in the worst‑case). The error is bounded by

\|\Theta(z_k) - A^k\Theta(z_0)\| \le \sum_{\ell=0}^{k-1} \|A^{k-1-\ell}\| \|\varepsilon_\ell\| \le \sum_{\ell=0}^{k-1} C_1 \sin\theta_1 \|\Theta(z_\ell)\| .

Since the Lyapunov‑stabilised dynamics keep trajectories within a compact set, there exists a constant $C_2$ such that $\|\Theta(z_\ell)\| \le C_2 \|\Theta(z_0)\|$ for all $\ell$. Therefore the sum is at most $k \, C_1 C_2 \sin\theta_1 \|\Theta(z_0)\|$. Taking $C = C_1 C_2$ yields the stated bound. $\square$

---

C.6 Proof of Theorem 6.6 (Conformal Coverage)

(a) One‑step split‑conformal prediction. Given a calibration set $\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}$ of independent residuals $R_i = \|y_{t+1} - \hat y_{t+1}\|$, define the $1-\alpha$ quantile $\hat q$ as the $\lceil (|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}|+1)(1-\alpha)\rceil$-th smallest residual. For a new test point, the prediction interval $\hat C_{t+1} = [\hat y_{t+1} \pm \hat q]$ satisfies $\mathbb{P}(y_{t+1}\in\hat C_{t+1}) \ge 1-\alpha$ exactly, provided the residuals are exchangeable (true for one‑step‑ahead IID setting). This follows from standard results (Vovk et al., 2005; Fasel et al., 2025).

(b) Multi‑step with dependence. For a multi‑step rollout, the residuals are not exchangeable because they are correlated over time. Assuming the process is β‑mixing, block‑conformal prediction divides the calibration sequence into consecutive blocks of length $L$, treats the block residuals as approximately exchangeable, and constructs intervals that over time achieve coverage $1-\alpha$ asymptotically as $T\to\infty$ (Chernozhukov et al., 2018). Alternatively, adaptive conformal inference (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) adjusts the quantile online, maintaining approximate coverage even for non‑exchangeable sequences. Both methods provide the asymptotic guarantee stated in the theorem. $\square$

