Appendix E — Computational Complexity and Verification Details

· S‑MDL expansion cost: The mutual coherence computation is $\mathcal{O}(K^2 N)$ per expansion check; with $K\sim200$ and $N\sim1000$ it is negligible compared to training. The ensemble standard deviation $\sigma_{\text{dyn}}$ is obtained cheaply from the 5 decoders.
· Triggered verification overhead:
  · dReal counterexample search runs every $p$ epochs on a small grid ($\le 10^3$ points) and adds minimal overhead ($<1\%$).
  · α,β‑CROWN is called only a handful of times during Phase 2; each call may take minutes on a GPU, but the total training time remains dominated by gradient descent. The strategy reduces verification cost by an estimated factor of 10–50 compared to verifying every epoch.
· Manifold freezing: Freezing and unfreezing encoder weights adds no computational cost; it is merely a gradient‑flow gating. The warm‑start phase trains only $1$–$10$ new coefficients for a few epochs, which is $O(1)$ relative to a full epoch.
· Conformal calibration: Split‑conformal calibration uses a held‑out set of size $N_{\text{cal}}\approx 1000$; quantile computation is $\mathcal{O}(N_{\text{cal}}\log N_{\text{cal}})$, negligible.
· Lyapunov certification: The low‑dimensional verified subsystem is limited to $k\le10$; beyond that, compositional verification remains future work.

